00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:06.750
May I never live to see my wife, honest.

00:21:07.590 --> 00:21:08.310
What's your business?

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:10.160
I'm in between right now.

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:12.480
Yeah, in between the devil and the deep blue sea.

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:14.200
Two felony convictions.

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:14.870
Perot.

00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:16.310
No.

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:17.830
Where did you find the satchel?

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:23.290
No, no, no, no, no.

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:24.190
The satchel.

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:34.710
Sometimes comic, sometimes tragic, the search went on.

00:21:36.460 --> 00:21:40.290
And like the other parts, it found its way to the weapons laboratory at Los Alamos,

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:42.950
where the display was taking on frightening proportions.

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:45.260
Six parts, six guesses.

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:47.420
Four of them exactly the same, even the weight.

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:51.900
That means they have four prize packages planned, at least.

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:56.790
And the other two, totally different sections, but all part of the same pattern.

00:21:58.560 --> 00:21:59.860
Makes three different parts altogether.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.750
Any idea how many would take to make up at least one of the bombs?

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:08.100
No way to tell yet. They'd still need a critical mass.

00:22:08.810 --> 00:22:13.070
The heart of the bomb, Mr. Williams. The actual element that produces the nuclear chain reaction.

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:15.910
Either plutonium or uranium-235.

00:22:16.940 --> 00:22:21.900
And that's it? It would be good to know how close they are to the real thing.

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:23.170
Well, that's your department.

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:32.080
How's it going?

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:33.920
Same spiel, different words.

00:22:34.430 --> 00:22:35.890
I got stopped by another newspaper man.

00:22:36.030 --> 00:22:37.590
I don't know how much longer we'll be able to stall them.

00:22:37.750 --> 00:22:38.770
As long as we have to.

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:41.150
We've enough to do without throwing the public into a panic.

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:41.890
It's getting tougher.

00:22:42.070 --> 00:22:43.850
We've got them all the way from Virginia to New Mexico.

00:22:44.070 --> 00:22:45.590
You can add a fan of to Connecticut, too.

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:46.290
What?

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:49.420
This time it's one of our own subs docked in at New London.

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:53.800
With uranium welded onto the hull below the waterline.

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:55.580
Uranium?

00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:59.500
How soon does Dr. Hallman figure they'll be ready for that?

00:22:59.830 --> 00:22:59.970
Two minutes.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.980
soon. And here's another little item to mull over. The stuff was removed in spite of the

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:08.450
guards. No one saw anything? Just the wound in the ship. A Geiger counter told them what

00:23:08.450 --> 00:23:14.100
had been there. Where's the sub in from? Marseille, France. Was that the only port you touched

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:19.690
the last time out? No. Two others before France. Since we haven't been able to make any contact

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:28.560
locally. Why not try it through the source? What source? The way I see it. France. Why

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:29.340
pinpoint France?

00:23:30.890 --> 00:23:32.990
Because that's where the belly of the octopus lives.

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.030
Look at the tentacles and where they're reaching from.

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:38.000
Marseille, France.

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:41.720
The last port the sub touched before coming here with uranium on the hull.

00:23:42.180 --> 00:23:44.300
Previous to that, docked at Hove and Lisbon.

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:47.940
What about the metal suitcase we picked out of that hot rod crash in New Mexico?

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:53.180
We've since learned it happened ten days after a French boat with a Marseille registration docked at Tampico.

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:55.400
But nothing says the boy was in Tampico.

00:23:55.670 --> 00:23:57.590
What about the sailor we picked up with one of the parts?

00:23:58.090 --> 00:23:59.970
He was shipped home from somewhere in southern France.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.420
I hadn't even stationed in North Africa before that.

00:24:02.690 --> 00:24:04.750
Just the same, it adds up to a common denominator.

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:07.610
If there's any one place the belly of the octopus is cradled,

00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:08.550
it could be right there.

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:11.780
So you figure a roping job might do it, huh?

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:13.160
Partly.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:15.080
All right, let's hear your idea.

00:24:16.310 --> 00:24:17.870
Order the sub back to Marseille.

00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:19.620
One of our men on it.

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:20.220
Yes, sir.

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:22.660
Might just be they'll try to use the sub again.

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:25.240
Sounds reasonable.

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:27.180
Who have we got familiar with Navy?

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:28.610
Oh, Lowman?

00:24:28.940 --> 00:24:29.300
Sperry?

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:29.700
Goldman?

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:35.900
pick navy instead of air force well they all salute the same yeah only one of them knows the

00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:40.780
difference between the bridge and the galley suppose I were assigned to some special duty

00:24:40.780 --> 00:24:48.690
like the preparation of a motion picture short on the operation of a sub what do you think you

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:53.500
might get by that way wouldn't have to take an active part as a sailor all right Williams

00:24:53.500 --> 00:24:58.480
get yourself another name and a couple of manuals on training films I'll see about a commission for

00:24:58.480 --> 00:24:58.620
you

00:25:21.850 --> 00:25:22.610
Reporting for duty, sir.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:23.740
At ease, Mr. Peters.

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:25.360
Nice to have you aboard.

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:26.100
Thank you, sir.

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:28.860
Training film on the operation of a sub, huh?

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:36.650
the equipment and the personnel yes we'll see that you get all the cooperation you need thank you sir

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:29.940
Say Waterford, way to southern France, crossroads for seamen of all.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:33.940
nations, a melting pot for some without a country to call their own.

00:27:01.790 --> 00:27:27.330
Several of our men are already stationed up by the submarine base.

00:27:28.100 --> 00:27:29.220
I want to get started right away.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:31.280
Audrey here will work with you.

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:33.600
You probably will need an interpreter.

00:27:34.380 --> 00:27:35.100
I sure will.

00:27:35.300 --> 00:27:36.460
We'll hit it off okay, pal.

00:27:38.330 --> 00:27:39.430
And that sounds real American.

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:40.090
It is.

00:27:40.270 --> 00:27:40.970
Young as New York.

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:42.030
The name's really Andy.

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:45.510
How was the trip across?

00:27:46.490 --> 00:27:46.890
Uneventful.

00:27:47.170 --> 00:27:48.810
Not much more to report from this end,

00:27:48.910 --> 00:27:52.090
except assurances that the French will give us a free hand in this case,

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:53.770
and they'll do all they can to help.

00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:56.710
Meanwhile, we got this list of names together for you.

00:27:57.850 --> 00:27:59.970
All possibilities for one reason.

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:03.280
and all known enemies of the United States.

00:28:04.280 --> 00:28:05.080
But nothing definite.

00:28:05.540 --> 00:28:08.080
Not enough to swap the waterfront hangout,

00:28:08.410 --> 00:28:10.540
le Café Henri, for a cell.

00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:12.960
The Café Henri.

00:28:13.610 --> 00:28:16.200
To serve excellent refreshments there.

00:28:17.510 --> 00:28:18.230
I'll let you know.

00:28:30.220 --> 00:28:37.940
On the bridge of Avignon

00:28:45.550 --> 00:28:54.570
Lolli dans l'Avignon

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:57.520
Lolli danse, lolli danse

00:28:57.520 --> 00:28:59.640
On the bridge of Avignon

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:04.260
In the dance, all the time, the good gentlemen still

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:11.680
STILL

00:29:54.910 --> 00:29:55.630
You're a kind of musician.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:01.160
The music makes a guy homesick.

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:02.180
Been over long?

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:04.540
One day to be exact.

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:05.460
How long are you gonna stay?

00:30:06.220 --> 00:30:07.040
I don't know for sure.

00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:08.080
Buy you a drink?

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:09.190
Got one.

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:13.120
Me about that clarinet player.

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:14.160
Buzz Olin?

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:16.600
He's up there with Benny Goodman and Artie Shaw.

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:18.610
He plays real great.

00:30:18.670 --> 00:30:19.530
I wouldn't mind meeting him.

00:30:20.570 --> 00:30:21.550
Oh, no sooner said than done.

00:30:22.240 --> 00:30:25.470
Come on, you've got an admirer.

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:26.970
Hi, you play?

00:30:27.380 --> 00:30:28.360
No, I just listen.

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:29.740
Good, I don't like competition.

00:30:30.250 --> 00:30:31.820
Sit down, join the party.

00:30:32.270 --> 00:30:35.030
Dave, come over and have a drink with us.

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:39.000
Excuse me, you gotta keep the customers happy.

00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:43.920
One of the prettiest reasons Americans come to France.

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:45.190
Enchanté, officier.

00:30:46.210 --> 00:30:46.670
How do you do?

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:48.630
Don't get any ideas. Leo hears her husband.

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:50.680
Where you from, friend?

00:30:51.060 --> 00:30:51.960
Brooklyn or Texas?

00:30:52.700 --> 00:30:52.900
Neither.

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:54.410
You see, honey?

00:30:54.550 --> 00:30:56.410
Not all Americans come from Brooklyn and Texas.

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:15.710
Musician. Played Italian, French, Riviera. Paris.

00:31:16.470 --> 00:31:17.810
Now, Café en Rhin, Marseille.

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:20.960
Background. Best picture.

00:31:21.940 --> 00:31:24.600
Copy for his passport photo. Keep on all Americans over here.

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:25.640
You mind if I borrow it?

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:29.720
What about Dave Norton?

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:32.160
the leader of the quartet, the one Olin worked for.

00:31:38.530 --> 00:31:40.310
Studied music here under the GI Bill of Rights.

00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:43.490
Stayed over. Complete dossier, clean slate.

00:31:45.240 --> 00:31:47.040
Olin was very friendly with the Waynes.

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:49.480
Might as well check on that while we're at it.

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:50.120
First name?

00:31:51.030 --> 00:31:52.990
Leo. Hers is Margo.

00:31:58.540 --> 00:31:58.780
The better.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:06.580
J.O. Wayne. Private. Signal Corps. Engineering degree MIT. Married to a Bordeaux girl, Margot Quattrolle.

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:11.370
His father was a French collaborationist. Wayne is 29. No visible means of support.

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:15.860
Suspected of subversive activity in the Army. His honorable discharge.

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:29.740
Any pictures on them? Captain, I'll see that these are returned to you.

00:32:32.430 --> 00:32:34.710
Now, this is important, Dave. I'm trying to get a line on Olin.

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:37.390
Well, he was a kind of a moody character.

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:40.440
I don't mean that. When did he tell you he was leaving?

00:32:41.010 --> 00:32:42.190
Well, that's just it. He didn't.

00:32:42.970 --> 00:32:46.060
I wouldn't have known about it, except I owed him a couple days' salary.

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:48.580
Did he have any reason? Anyone sick at home or anything?

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:51.620
Ah, not buzz. Just got restless.

00:32:52.750 --> 00:32:56.200
Had a good, solid job. Had lots of jobs.

00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:57.820
Never stuck to any of them.

00:32:58.600 --> 00:32:59.600
Did you take him to the plane?

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:02.610
Yeah, and me and the Waynes. They're his pals.

00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:03.580
Very close.

00:33:03.990 --> 00:33:06.190
Well, she's the one who first gave me the knockdown of the guy.

00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:08.640
As a matter of fact, he left me in a spot.

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:10.820
Not even a chance to replace him.

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:15.440
I tried to talk him out of it up to the minute the plane left early this morning.

00:33:17.210 --> 00:33:18.270
Did he have much luggage?

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:19.820
Buzz? Nah.

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:23.140
Only one who's assumed is back and a couple licorice sticks.

00:33:23.900 --> 00:33:26.460
Do you happen to know where he bought the metal case he carries his clarinet in?

00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:28.980
I haven't any idea.

00:33:30.990 --> 00:33:32.530
Have the Waynes been around since he left?

00:33:33.140 --> 00:33:35.630
Not tonight, but they usually drop in about 11.

00:33:37.020 --> 00:33:39.830
One thing more, Dave. I haven't been here.

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:41.450
Gotcha.

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:14.160
Slippery boy. Check all the luggage shops.

00:34:14.500 --> 00:34:16.440
See if we can find out who made that clarinet case

00:34:16.440 --> 00:34:26.170
for Mr. Olam. While Williams, Andre

00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:28.070
and the French police search the noisy streets

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:29.360
in our waterfront.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:31.940
double-checking every luggage maker in Marseille.

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:34.620
Back home, the fuse was burning closer.

00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:40.630
More parts, pins in the map.

00:34:43.240 --> 00:34:43.880
More questioning.

00:34:45.500 --> 00:34:47.080
And more midnight oil being burned.

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:03.700
This was the place they'd fall.

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:06.410
This was the man who made the metal cases used to sneak out

00:35:06.410 --> 00:35:07.790
and parts into the United States.

00:35:08.210 --> 00:35:09.610
A client gave me the plan.

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:11.270
He ordered 48.

00:35:11.530 --> 00:35:11.890
His name?

00:35:13.100 --> 00:35:13.600
I don't call him.

00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:15.550
Looks like you've run into something here.

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:18.130
He made up 48 specifications for a customer.

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:20.350
Ask him where he delivered them.

00:35:20.880 --> 00:35:21.800
Where would he deliver the police?

00:35:22.220 --> 00:35:23.800
He came to the search with a truck.

00:35:24.500 --> 00:35:25.760
No good, truck.

00:35:28.140 --> 00:35:29.980
Ask him if the customer that ordered the cases is one of them.

00:35:50.380 --> 00:35:51.860
One he outfitted for a clarinet.

00:35:54.940 --> 00:35:55.760
What did he say?

00:35:56.030 --> 00:35:57.330
Just that he cooperates with the police.

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:06.080
All he could tell us was that someone had ordered 48 identical metal cases.

00:36:06.550 --> 00:36:09.430
And he identified Buzz Olin as having ordered an additional four.

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:10.930
But he did identify Leo Wayne.

00:36:11.340 --> 00:36:12.140
No, he didn't.

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:14.680
But with Olin's disappearance, Leo Wayne is our only lead.

00:36:14.920 --> 00:36:16.260
Just because they were friends?

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:20.230
Add that to the fact that Leo and Margot Wayne were seen around the cafe continually.

00:36:21.120 --> 00:36:23.600
And their friends were all a part of that special list you gave me.

00:36:24.210 --> 00:36:26.410
Also, he's been seen hanging around the submarine area.

00:36:27.220 --> 00:36:29.980
And it's more than just a coincidence that Buzz Olin ordered four...

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:34.040
identical metal cases when he only has one clarinet why don't you pick Wayne up then

00:36:35.180 --> 00:36:39.680
because we don't have enough on him but I'm more than convinced that he's tied into this

00:36:39.760 --> 00:36:43.340
there were no metal suitcases in fact nothing of any importance when we searched the department

00:36:43.340 --> 00:36:48.020
then what was he doing hanging around the sub the subject curiosity hundreds of people come

00:36:48.020 --> 00:36:53.100
down to the dock every day look if Buzz Olin ordered four identical metal cases then he or

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:56.460
a friend must have had something to do with sneaking the uranium onto the hull of the ship

00:36:56.460 --> 00:36:59.980
well if it is so they must have got someone to work with someone right on the

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:07.230
submarine exactly how do we draw them out just wait there's no time we've got

00:37:07.230 --> 00:37:13.880
to force them into the open how they probably think the submarine will remain

00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:20.520
here for a while we'll have it ordered back unexpectedly if they plan to use

00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:24.690
the sub again they'll have to do it right away they'd be fools maybe they

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:25.690
think we are sure

